{
  "note": "The shape of the construction, as a row of slots.",
  "why": "A clause pattern is a shape, and a paragraph is a bad way to show a shape. 'The conjugated verb moves to the end of the clause' is accurate and still leaves the learner to picture it; weil + Subject + … + Verb is the same claim in the form it actually has. Written here rather than derived, because deriving it needs a parser and a wrong pattern teaches wrong grammar.",
  "provenance": "authored-for-this-project",
  "caveat": "Each one restates a rule the topic already states in prose. Nothing here adds a rule the source does not make, and a topic whose construction is not schematic carries no formula rather than a forced one.",
  "syntax": "A slot is \"text\" or \"text:role\", role being one of conj, verb, prep, q — the same four classes the example highlights use, so a connector is the same colour in the formula and in the sentence below it. German literals are quoted as themselves; the placeholders are in the interface language.",
  "formulas": {
    "g-daf-a1-subject-position": [
      { "caption": "the subject first", "slots": ["Subject", "Verb:verb", "…"] },
      { "caption": "something else first — the verb still comes second", "slots": ["Time or place", "Verb:verb", "Subject", "…"] }
    ],
    "g-daf-a1-word-order-for-positive-statements-w-que": [
      { "caption": "statement", "slots": ["Subject", "Verb:verb", "…"] },
      { "caption": "W-question", "slots": ["W-word:q", "Verb:verb", "Subject", "…"] },
